// A program declares its own type-position operator: a `syntax
// type_construct` rule and its level (PROP-019, `TypeInfix`). The type
// `i64 ~> i64` parses as `fn<i64, i64>`, but the seed then tries to lower the
// rule's `quote { .. }` template as code and stops with SEED001 (the same
// happens for a rule without an operator, `(maybe<T>) => quote { option<$T> }`).
//
// Expected, worked by hand: apply((n) => n + 1, 2) = 2 + 1 = 3.
pub syntax type_construct {
    (A ~> B) => quote { fn<$A, $B> };
}
comptime {
    TypeInfix("~>", ::Arrow, ::Right);
}
fn apply(f: i64 ~> i64, x: i64) -> i64 => f(x);
fn main() {
    print(apply((n) => n + 1, 2));
}
